s="\"P442.dvi (https://real.mtak.hu/20177/1/ajc_v59_1_p211_217_u_181146.177653.pdf)\\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.7 years ago; Hence it is not even possible to find \\\\(n - 1\\\\) disjoint transversals. ... An interesting counterpoint to Theorem 5 is the unresolved conjecture by Stein ([9], see also [10]) that any \\\\((n-1)\\\\times n\\\\) row-latin rectangle has a transversal.\\nHence it is not even possible to find \\\\(n - 1\\\\) disjoint transversals. For \\\\(n \\\\neq 6\\\\) we found \\\\(n - 1\\\\) disjoint transversals in all the candidates that we tested.\\n\\nMoreover, we checked exhaustively and found that every \\\\(7 \\\\times 8\\\\) latin rectangle can be decomposed into transversals. We also tried to generalise the structure of some small examples to \\\\(n > 8\\\\), but without success. Thus it remains an interesting open question as to whether there are only finitely many examples of the type sought by Häggkvist and Johansson.\\n\\nIf we relax the conditions slightly it becomes easy to build infinite families of examples, as our next two results show.\\n\\n**Theorem 5**  *For any \\\\(n \\\\ge 3\\\\) there exists an \\\\((n-1)\\\\times n\\\\) row-latin rectangle that cannot be decomposed into transversals.*\\n\\n**Proof:** Let \\\\(R\\\\) be the row-latin rectangle consisting of \\\\(n-2\\\\) rows that are copies of \\\\([1,2,\\\\ldots,n]\\\\), followed by a single row \\\\([2,3,\\\\ldots,n-2,n-1,1,n]\\\\). Now consider any \\\\(x \\\\in \\\\{1,2,\\\\ldots,n-1\\\\}\\\\). A transversal that includes symbol \\\\(x\\\\) from the last row cannot use any entry in column \\\\(x\\\\), so it has to use an entry in the last column. However there are only \\\\(n-2\\\\) entries in the last column outside the last row, and these have to be paired with the \\\\(n-1\\\\) different values of \\\\(x\\\\) in any decomposition of \\\\(R\\\\) into transversals. Hence \\\\(R\\\\) cannot be decomposed into transversals. □\\n\\nAn interesting counterpoint to Theorem 5 is the unresolved conjecture by Stein ([9], see also [10]) that any \\\\((n-1)\\\\times n\\\\) row-latin rectangle has a transversal.\\n\\n**Theorem 6**  *For any odd \\\\(n \\\\ge 3\\\\) there exists an \\\\((n-1)\\\\times n\\\\) generalized latin rectangle that cannot be decomposed into transversals.*\\n\\n**Proof:** Since \\\\(n-1\\\\) is even, there exists a latin square \\\\(L\\\\) of order \\\\(n-1\\\\) that contains no transversal [10]. Adjoin to \\\\(L\\\\) a new column consisting of symbols that are distinct from each other and from the symbols in \\\\(L\\\\). This produces an \\\\((n-1)\\\\times n\\\\) generalized latin rectangle. It cannot be decomposed into transversals, since any such decomposition would have to include one transversal of \\\\(L\\\\). □--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA six-neuron counterexample to the target-free clique conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.21396)\\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Date: Thu Jul 23 14:58:30 2026\\n\\nThe target-free clique conjecture asserts that the supports of stable fixed points of a nondegenerate combinatorial threshold-linear network (CTLN) are exactly its target-free cliques: bidirected cliques for which no outside vertex receives an edge from every clique vertex.\\nTitle: A six-neuron counterexample to the target-free clique conjecture\\nAuthors: Jesse Geneson\\nDate: Thu Jul 23 14:58:30 2026\\n\\nThe target-free clique conjecture asserts that the supports of stable fixed points of a nondegenerate combinatorial threshold-linear network (CTLN) are exactly its target-free cliques: bidirected cliques for which no outside vertex receives an edge from every clique vertex. We give an explicit six-neuron counterexample. For every sufficiently small $\\\\varepsilon>0$, the CTLN defined by one fixed graph at $δ=29\\\\varepsilon/25$ is nondegenerate and has a stable fixed point with nonclique full support. Its values of $q=δ(1-\\\\varepsilon)/\\\\varepsilon$ tend to $29/25$. In the complementary direction, for any CTLN on $n\\\\geq3$ vertices, we prove that in the parameter range \\\\[ q\\\\geq n-2-\\\\frac{n-3}{2}\\\\varepsilon, \\\\] no nonclique support can satisfy both the fixed-point positivity and linear stability conditions. Consequently, throughout this range, every nondegenerate CTLN has exactly its target-free cliques as supports of stable fixed points. In particular, this holds when $\\\\varepsilon\\\\leqδ/(δ+n-2)$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe target-free clique conjecture for combinatorial threshold-linear networks | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/371376/the-target-free-clique-conjecture-for-combinatorial-threshol)\\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; # The target-free clique conjecture for combinatorial threshold-linear networks ... The target-free clique conjecture.\\n\\n# The target-free clique conjecture for combinatorial threshold-linear networks\\n\\n1 year old · traced to 2025\\n\\nLet $G$G be a directed graph on $n$n nodes, and let $W=W(G;\\\\varepsilon,\\\\delta)$W=W(G;ε,δ) be the associated combinatorial threshold-linear network (CTLN). A subset $\\\\sigma\\\\subseteq[n]$σ⊆[n] is a clique if $G|_{\\\\sigma}$G∣σ​ has all bidirectional edges between distinct vertices. A vertex $v\\\\notin\\\\sigma$v∈/σ is a target of $\\\\sigma$σ if every $j\\\\in\\\\sigma$j∈σ has $j\\\\to v$j→v in $G$G, and $\\\\sigma$σ is target-free if it has no external target. Assume\\n\\n$$\\\\delta>0,\\\\qquad 0<\\\\varepsilon<\\\\frac{\\\\delta}{\\\\delta+1}.$$δ>0,0<ε<δ+1 δ​.\\n\\nThe target-free clique conjecture. A subset $\\\\sigma$σ is the support of a stable fixed point if and only if $\\\\sigma$σ is a target-free clique. Target-free cliques are known to support stable fixed points, and the converse has been proved in several special cases, including oriented and symmetric graphs, networks with at most four nodes, and certain parameter ranges; the general statement remains open.\\n\\nReferences\\n\\n  1. Jesse Geneson, “Stable non-minimal fixed points of threshold-linear networks”, arXiv:2511.05517 (2025).\\n\\ncombinatoricsdynamical-systemsthreshold-linear-networks\\n\\nPosted 2 months ago\\n\\n## Progress summary\\n\\nRefresh Never refreshed\\n\\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\\n\\n## Solutions 0\\n\\n[Button: Post a solution]\\n\\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nA six-neuron counterexample to the target-free clique conjecture - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2607.21396v1)\\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last week; Published: 2026-07-23 ... The target-free clique conjecture asserts that the supports of stable fixed points of a nondegenerate combinatorial threshold-linear network (CTLN) are exactly its target-free cliques: bidirected cliques for which no outside vertex receives an edge from every clique vertex.\\n\\n## A six-neuron counterexample to the target-free clique conjecture\\n\\nJesse Geneson\\n\\nPublished: 2026-07-23\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nPublication:\\n\\ncs.DM math.CO\\n\\ngraph\\n\\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\\n\\n### Abstract\\n\\nThe target-free clique conjecture asserts that the supports of stable fixed points of a nondegenerate combinatorial threshold-linear network (CTLN) are exactly its target-free cliques: bidirected cliques for which no outside vertex receives an edge from every clique vertex. We give an explicit six-neuron counterexample. For every sufficiently small $\\\\varepsilon>0$, the CTLN defined by one fixed graph at $δ=29\\\\varepsilon/25$ is nondegenerate and has a stable fixed point with nonclique full support. Its values of $q=δ(1-\\\\varepsilon)/\\\\varepsilon$ tend to $29/25$. In the complementary direction, for any CTLN on $n\\\\geq3$ vertices, we prove that in the parameter range \\\\[ q\\\\geq n-2-\\\\frac{n-3}{2}\\\\varepsilon, \\\\] no nonclique support can satisfy both the fixed-point positivity and linear stability conditions. Consequently, throughout this range, every nondegenerate CTLN has exactly its target-free cliques as supports of stable fixed points. In particular, this holds when $\\\\varepsilon\\\\leqδ/(δ+n-2)$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nlatintransv.dvi (https://cs.nyu.edu/~spencer/papers/latintransv.pdf)\\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Given an \\\\(n \\\\times n\\\\) matrix \\\\(A = [a_{ij}]\\\\), a transversal of \\\\(A\\\\) is a set of elements, one from each row and one from each column. ... There have been more conjectures than theorems on Latin transversals in the literature. ... Any \\\\(n \\\\times n\\\\) matrix in which no element appears more than \\\\(s\\\\) times contains \\\\(n\\\\) disjoint latin transversals provided \\\\(s \\\\leq \\\\epsilon n\\\\) (for \\\\(\\\\epsilon\\\\), an absolute constant \\\\(\\\\ll 1\\\\)).\\n# Covering with Latin Transversals\\n\\nNoga Alon¹, Joel Spencer² and Prasad Tetali³\\n\\n(first draft)\\n\\n## 1 Introduction\\n\\nGiven an \\\\(n \\\\times n\\\\) matrix \\\\(A = [a_{ij}]\\\\), a transversal of \\\\(A\\\\) is a set of elements, one from each row and one from each column. A transversal is a *Latin transversal* if no two elements are the same. There have been more conjectures than theorems on Latin transversals in the literature. Erdős et al. [6] provide a good overview of the work done on transversals. Previous work can be found also in [10] and [4].\\n\\nRecently, Erdős and Spencer showed [5] that there always exists a latin transversal in any \\\\(n \\\\times n\\\\) matrix in which no element appears more than \\\\(s\\\\) times, for \\\\(s \\\\leq (n - 1)/16\\\\). Here we show that, in fact, all the elements of the matrix can be partitioned into Latin transversals, provided \\\\(n\\\\) is a power of 2 and no element appears more than \\\\(\\\\epsilon n\\\\) times for some fixed \\\\(\\\\epsilon > 0\\\\).\\n\\n**Theorem 1** *Let \\\\(n\\\\) be \\\\(2^m\\\\). Any \\\\(n \\\\times n\\\\) matrix in which no element appears more than \\\\(s\\\\) times contains \\\\(n\\\\) disjoint latin transversals provided \\\\(s \\\\leq \\\\epsilon n\\\\) (for \\\\(\\\\epsilon\\\\), an absolute constant \\\\(\\\\ll 1\\\\)).*\\n\\nThe assumption that \\\\(n\\\\) is a power of 2 can be weakened, but at the moment we are unable to prove the theorem for all values of \\\\(n\\\\). On the other hand, our proof can be easily modified to prove the existence of many pairwise disjoint transversals in any \\\\(n\\\\) by \\\\(n\\\\) matrix in which no entry appears more than \\\\(\\\\epsilon n\\\\) times, without any restriction on \\\\(n\\\\). Therefore our method implies a strengthening of the result of [5] for any \\\\(n\\\\), (apart from the actual value of the constant \\\\(\\\\epsilon\\\\)).\\n\\nWe prove the above theorem in a more general framework, stated in terms of graphs.\\n\\n¹ Bellcore, Morristown, NJ 07960 and Department of Mathematics, Sackler Faculty of Exact Sciences, Tel Aviv University, Tel Aviv, Israel. Research supported in part by a USA-Israel BSF grant  \\n² Department of Computer Science, Courant Institute of Mathematical Sciences, New York, NY 10012. Research supported in part by a USA-Israel BSF grant  \\n³ Visitor, DIMACS center, Rutgers University, P.O. Box 1179–Busch Campus, Piscataway, NJ 08855\\n\\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAnswers to Questions by Denes on Latin Power Sets (https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3A78a3c1d3-2927-4ee6-ad16-a26b66380af3/files/me30d97ff8dd0e2cb03f477166400a9a3)\\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 25.2 years ago; D´enes has used Latin power sets in an attempt to settle a conjecture by Hall and Paige on complete ... A transversal of a row-Latin square of order n is a selection of n entries of the square ... (iii) L can be decomposed into disjoint transversals,\\nSuppose\\nthat p and q are two permutations in the symmetric group Sn. Let Li j = k if and only if\\ngp(i) ⊗gq( j) = gk. The matrix L = (Li j) is a Latin square, and we say it is based on G. In\\nthe special case when g1 = ε and both p and q are the identity permutation we say that L is a\\nCayley table for G. Note that the ﬁrst row and column of a Cayley table are in natural order,\\nbut the remainder of the square depends on the enumeration chosen for the elements of G.\\nAlso, note that the squares based on a given group form an isotopy class.\\nA complete mapping of G is a permutation σ of the elements of G such that the map τ\\ndeﬁned by τ(x) = x⊗σ(x) is also a permutation of the elements of G. See [3] for background\\non all the concepts just introduced and for a proof of this standard result.\\nTHEOREM 1. Let L be a Latin square based on a ﬁnite group G. The following statements\\nare equivalent.\\n(i) G has a complete mapping,\\n(ii) L has a transversal,\\n(iii) L can be decomposed into disjoint transversals,\\n(iv) there exists a Latin square orthogonal to L.\\nHall and Paige [9] attempted to identify conditions under which a ﬁnite group would have\\na complete mapping. They made the following conjecture.\\n0195–6698/01/071009 + 12\\n$35.00/0\\nc⃝2001 Academic Press--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nConsidered but not curated · AI Proof Watch (https://aiproofwatch.com/sources/considered?page=30)\\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; ### The Target-Free Clique Conjecture for Threshold-Linear Networks ... Screening recorded: 2026-10-17 · Decision reviewed: 2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z ... The original tight-Hamilton-cycle paper states a quasirandom 3-graph theorem but the readable manuscript contains no AI disclosure for its proof; the batch metadata supplies no central-origin evidence.\\n### The Target-Free Clique Conjecture for Threshold-Linear Networks\\n\\nVibeMathed\\n\\nOn hold after review\\n\\nThe acknowledgments say Codex and Claude Code helped with proof exploration and criticism, but do not identify a model-origin six-neuron counterexample or central proof step.\\n\\nScreening recorded: 2026-10-17 · Decision reviewed: 2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z\\n\\n### Four-Terminal Planar Case of the Dinitz-Garg-Goemans Cost Conjecture\\n\\nVibeMathed\\n\\nRelated curated record\\n\\nThe example gives a small planar witness and a stronger lower bound for this rounding problem.\\n\\nScreening recorded: 2026-10-17 · Decision reviewed: 2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z\\n\\n  * AI-assisted planar example strengthens an unsplittable-flow bound\\n\\n### Unconditional One-Bit Unclonable Encryption\\n\\nVibeMathed\\n\\nRelated curated record\\n\\nThe construction combines unconditional security, efficiency and exponentially small cloning advantage for one-bit messages.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nDatabase | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/database?tag=transversals)\\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * ## Brualdi–Ryser–Stein conjecture for partial transversals in Latin squares ...   * ## The disjoint-transversal conjecture for row-Latin rectangles\\n0 0 8\\n\\n  * ## Brualdi–Ryser–Stein conjecture for partial transversals in Latin squares\\n\\nAbout 8 years old\\n\\nBrualdi–Ryser–Stein conjecture. Every $n\\\\times n$n×n Latin square has a partial transversal of size $n-1$n−1.\\n\\nopenlatin-squaresrainbow-matchingstransversals\\n\\n0 0 7\\n\\n  * ## Brualdi's conjecture for near transversals in latin hypercubes\\n\\nAbout 17 years old\\n\\nA latin hypercube of dimension $d$d and order $n$n is a $d$d-dimensional array filled with $n$n symbols so that every line contains all symbols exactly once. A near transversal is a co…\\n\\nopenlatin-hypercubestransversals\\n\\n0 0 8\\n\\n  * ## Stein's conjecture on transversals in Latin arrays\\n\\nAbout 10 years old\\n\\nStein's conjecture. Let $R$R be an $(n-1)\\\\times n$(n−1)×n Latin array. Then $R$R contains a transversal.\\n\\nopencombinatoricslatin-arraystransversals\\n\\n0 0 9\\n\\n  * ## The Brualdi–Ryser conjecture for partial transversals in latin squares\\n\\nAbout 17 years old\\n\\nBrualdi–Ryser conjecture. Every latin square $L$L of order $n$n possesses a near transversal, and if $n$n is odd then $L$L possesses a transversal.\\n\\nopencombinatoricslatin-squarestransversals\\n\\n0 0 11\\n\\n  * ## Drisko's uniqueness conjecture for transversal-free row-Latin rectangles\\n\\nAt least 27 years old\\n\\nDrisko's conjecture. Either $A$A has a transversal, or $A$A can be transformed into $R_{2n-2,n}$R 2 n−2,n​ by permuting rows, columns, and symbols.\\n\\nopenlatin-rectanglestransversals\\n\\n0 0 11\\n\\n  * ## The disjoint-transversal conjecture for row-Latin rectangles\\n\\nAbout 28 years old\\n\\nDisjoint-transversal conjecture. If $m\\\\ge2n-1$m≥2 n−1, then $A$A has $m-(n-1)$m−(n−1) pairwise disjoint transversals.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAUSTRALASIAN JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS (https://ajc.maths.uq.edu.au/pdf/59/ajc_v59_p211.pdf)\\nciteturn0search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; even possible to ﬁnd n −1 disjoint transversals. ... An interesting counterpoint to Theorem 5 is the unresolved conjecture by Stein\\n([9], see also [10]) that any (n −1) × n row-latin rectangle has a transversal.\\nThus it remains an interesting open\\nquestion as to whether there are only ﬁnitely many examples of the type sought by\\nH¨aggkvist and Johansson.\\nIf we relax the conditions slightly it becomes easy to build inﬁnite families of\\nexamples, as our next two results show.\\nTheorem 5 For any n ⩾3 there exists an (n−1)×n row-latin rectangle that cannot\\nbe decomposed into transversals.\\nProof:\\nLet R be the row-latin rectangle consisting of n −2 rows that are copies of\\n[1, 2, . . . , n], followed by a single row [2, 3, . . ., n −2, n −1, 1, n]. Now consider any\\nx ∈{1, 2, . . ., n −1}. A transversal that includes symbol x from the last row cannot\\nuse any entry in column x, so it has to use an entry in the last column. However\\nthere are only n −2 entries in the last column outside the last row, and these have\\nto be paired with the n −1 diﬀerent values of x in any decomposition of R into\\ntransversals. Hence R cannot be decomposed into transversals.\\n2\\nAn interesting counterpoint to Theorem 5 is the unresolved conjecture by Stein\\n([9], see also [10]) that any (n −1) × n row-latin rectangle has a transversal.\\nTheorem 6 For any odd n ⩾3 there exists an (n−1)×n generalized latin rectangle\\nthat cannot be decomposed into transversals.\\nProof:\\nSince n −1 is even, there exists a latin square L of order n −1 that con-\\ntains no transversal [10]. Adjoin to L a new column consisting of symbols that are\\ndistinct from each other and from the symbols in L. This produces an (n −1) × n\\ngeneralized latin rectangle. It cannot be decomposed into transversals, since any\\nsuch decomposition would have to include one transversal of L.\\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\ntransurveyBCC.dvi (https://users.monash.edu.au/~iwanless/papers/transurveyBCC.pdf)\\nciteturn0search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.6 years ago; They claim the same method can be adapted to show the existence of a number of pairwise disjoint transversals in matrices of a similar type, but whose order need not be a power of 2.\\n\\nAkbari and Alireza [3] define l(n) to be the smallest integer such that there is a transversal in every n × n matrix that is row-latin and column-latin and contains at least l(n) different symbols. ... This conjecture (which remains open) does not generalise directly to all abelian groups of even order, as Theorem 10.8 shows.\\nTransversals in Latin Squares                                                                 28\\n\\n**Theorem 10.7** *If n ≤ 2m − 2 then L(m, n) ≤ n − 1.*\\n\\nVarious results have been shown using the probabilistic method. Erdős and Spencer [53] showed that L(n, n) ≥ (n − 1)/16. Fanai [60] took the direct generalisation to rectangular matrices, showing L(m, n) ≥ 1/8 n(n − 1)/(m + n − 2) + 1. Alon *et al.* [6] showed that there is a small ε > 0 such that if no symbol occurs more than ε2^m times in a 2^m × 2^m matrix, then the matrix can be decomposed into transversals. They claim the same method can be adapted to show the existence of a number of pairwise disjoint transversals in matrices of a similar type, but whose order need not be a power of 2.\\n\\nAkbari and Alireza [3] define l(n) to be the smallest integer such that there is a transversal in every n × n matrix that is row-latin and column-latin and contains at least l(n) different symbols. They show that l(1) = 1, l(2) = l(3) = 3, l(4) = 6, and l(5) ≥ 7. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nDatabase | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/database?sort=newest&tag=matroid-theory)\\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * ## The disjoint-transversal conjecture for row-Latin rectangles ... open latin-rectangles matroid-theory transversals\\nopengraph-theorymatroid-theoryrigidity-theory\\n\\n0 0 12\\n\\n  * ## The disjoint-independent-transversal conjecture for matroid-valued matrices\\n\\nAbout 28 years old\\n\\nDisjoint-independent-transversal conjecture. If $m\\\\ge2n-1$m≥2 n−1, then $A$A has $m-(n-1)$m−(n−1) pairwise disjoint ITs.\\n\\nopenindependent-transversalsmatroid-theory\\n\\n0 0 8\\n\\n  * ## The disjoint-transversal conjecture for row-Latin rectangles\\n\\nAbout 28 years old\\n\\nDisjoint-transversal conjecture. If $m\\\\ge2n-1$m≥2 n−1, then $A$A has $m-(n-1)$m−(n−1) pairwise disjoint transversals.\\n\\nopenlatin-rectanglesmatroid-theorytransversals\\n\\n0 0 8\\n\\n  * ## The homotopy-type conjecture for connected central arrangements\\n\\nAt least 27 years old\\n\\nLet ${\\\\mathcal A}$A be a central hyperplane arrangement with connected underlying matroid, and let $C({\\\\mathcal A})$C(A) denote its complement. Homotopy-type conjecture. The homotopy ty…\\n\\nopenhomotopy-theoryhyperplane-arrangementsmatroid-theory\\n\\n0 0 7\\n\\n  * ## The corrected Welsh conjecture on the possible numbers of matroid bases\\n\\nAbout 19 years old\\n\\nCorrected Welsh conjecture. For every triple $(n,r,b)$(n,r,b) such that $0 \\\\leq r \\\\leq n$0≤r≤n and $1 \\\\leq b \\\\leq \\\\binom{n}{r}$1≤b≤(r n​), there is a matroid of rank $r$r on $n$n elements with exactly $b$b…\\n\\nopenenumerative-combinatoricsmatroid-theory\\n\\n0 0 7\\n\\n  * ## Converse to the tropical-rank criterion for tropical bases\\n\\nAt least 19 years old\\n\\nLet $L$L be an $(n-k)$(n−k)-dimensional linear subspace of $K^n$K n whose tropical Plücker coordinates are all finite, and let $M\\\\in K^{m\\\\times n}$M∈K m×n, with $m\\\\geq k$m≥k, have nonzero rows in the…\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nSome Concepts in List Coloring (https://www.academia.edu/15206261/Some_Concepts_in_List_Coloring)\\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.9 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; By Lemma 2, G has a vertex v0 , such that there are 5 t t t t t t t Figure 1: A uniquely 3–list colorable planar graph at least k colors appeared at N (v0 ) in c. ... \\u0004 The following conjecture is about the structure of U3LC planar graphs which is motivated by the proposition above. ... Every U3LC planar graph has K4 as a subgraph.\\n For every planar graph G we have m(G) 6 4. By Lemma 1 the planar graph shown in Figure 1 is a U3LC graph for which the inequalities in Theorem 3 and Theorem 5 turn to be equalities. Furthermore we know 